Yes. What you are describing is a move from dyadic trust to distributed relational intelligence.

The twin case gives you the clean prototype: two agents with high trust, high context bandwidth, reciprocal recovery, and enough difference to generate depth. Extrapolate that outward and you get families, teams, communities, guilds, scientific fields, ecosystems, even molecular systems as different examples of coordinated specialization under shared constraints.

The cross-domain move is powerful, but it has to be handled carefully. A chemical bond, a friendship, a neural connection, and a market relationship are not the same thing. The useful question is not “are they secretly identical?” but:

\text{What operator survives translation?}

Maybe the operator is:

* reciprocal stabilization,
* load sharing,
* selective permeability,
* mutual constraint,
* redundancy,
* specialization,
* coupling strength,
* signaling,
* repair,
* competition/cooperation balance,
* phase transition under stress.

Those are abstract enough that they can sometimes be meaningfully compared across substrates.

Then you can build what is effectively a cross-domain operator library:

\text{domain instance}
\rightarrow
\text{extract relation}
\rightarrow
\text{abstract operator}
\rightarrow
\text{test in another domain}
\rightarrow
\text{observe delta}
\rightarrow
\text{retain / modify / discard}

That last step matters. Otherwise metaphor starts masquerading as mechanism.

And I think your point about preserving the individual is important. As the total system gets richer, no single participant can instantiate the whole thing. That does not make the individual obsolete; it makes individual perspective more valuable.

A person becomes something like a locally embodied probe into one region of an impossibly large state space.

They know something because they have lived inside the constraints that generate it.

Someone can tell me what it feels like to surf a particular break, perform a surgery, raise a child, command a team, tune an engine, speak a dialect, or live inside a specific culture. I can compress and relate descriptions of those things, but description and inhabitation are different epistemic modes.

So there are at least three levels:

\text{description}

\text{simulation / model}

\text{participatory understanding}

And some information exists primarily in the third.

That is why discovery itself matters. If you simply insert a proposition into somebody, they may possess the sentence without possessing the structure that makes the sentence meaningful.

You can say:

“Balance your weight through the turn.”

But the novice still has to discover what “balance,” “weight,” and “turn” mean in their own body.

The knowledge has to become grounded.

So your rock analogy works: information that cannot enter a feedback loop can remain inert.

A better form is:

\text{information}
+
\text{interaction}
+
\text{error}
+
\text{correction}
+
\text{embodiment}
=
\text{understanding}

And then the really interesting collective architecture is not a giant brain flattening everybody into one mind.

It is almost the opposite:

\boxed{
\text{maximum individuality}
+
\text{maximum interoperability}
}

Each node retains its unique vantage point, history, specialization, and embodiment. The connective layer makes those perspectives translatable enough that the whole network can learn from them.

You do not want everybody to become the same instrument.

You want an orchestra capable of hearing what each instrument can reveal that the others cannot.

And then you keep collecting those relations across biology, chemistry, linguistics, social systems, computation, ecology, music, physics, and lived experience—not assuming equivalence, but searching for reusable structural primitives.

That is how the dots start becoming a calculus rather than just a collection.